<Docs slug="vs-et" description="osewa compared with Eternal Terminal (ET): how each reconnects, measured on the same host, and what ET does that osewa doesn't.">
<p class="lede">Eternal Terminal (ET) is the closest tool to osewa: a reconnecting terminal with forwards and sessions that outlive the network. ET makes a TCP connection that can't die and replays what you missed. osewa keeps the screen on the host and sends you the current one.</p>
<p>This page comes from reading ET 7.1.1's source and running it beside osewa on one host.</p>

<h2>The main difference</h2>
<p>ET puts a layer under the TCP socket. Each side numbers what it sends and keeps it, encrypted, in a buffer. After a reconnect the two sides swap sequence numbers and resend whatever the other missed, so your terminal gets every byte, in order, as if the link had never dropped. Your terminal does the rendering, and its own scrollback holds the output.</p>
<p>osewa runs the program under libghostty-vt on the host, which keeps the screen. A reconnect sends that screen as one snapshot, however much you missed. The output in between stays on the host, in the session's scrollback, rather than in your terminal's.</p>
<p>That shapes what each does badly. While the link is down, ET holds up to 64 MiB of new output, then stops reading from the program, which pauses it until you're back. And a client that starts fresh, after a crash or a reboot, resets the stream: <code>et --attach</code> gets the shell back, but no replay and no screen, so you see only what comes next. osewa's cost is that it has to parse everything the program writes, and it can only show what its engine understands.</p>

